# toMap
Rational map defined by a linear system

- **Package:** [Cremona](../../Cremona/package/Cremona.md)
- **Class:** [MethodFunctionWithOptions](../../Core/type/MethodFunctionWithOptions.md)
- **Protected:** true
- **Mutable:** false
- **Defined at:** `/home/flux/.cache/yay/macaulay2/src/M2/M2/Macaulay2/packages/Cremona.m2:86:0-86:5`
- **Result type:** [RingMap](../../Core/type/RingMap.md)
- **Aliases:** `Cremona$toMap`

## Description
When the input represents a list of homogeneous elements $F_0,\ldots, F_m\in R=K[t_0,\ldots, t_n]/I$ of the same degree, then the method returns the ring map $\phi: K[x_0,\ldots, x_m] \to R$ that sends $x_i$ into $F_i$.

If a positive integer $d$ is passed to the option [Dominant](../../Cremona/symbol/Dominant.md), then the method returns the induced map on $K[x_0,\ldots, x_m]/J_d$, where $J_d$ is the ideal generated by all homogeneous elements of degree $d$ of the kernel of $\phi$ (in this case [kernel(RingMap,ZZ)](kernel_lpar_RingMap_cma_ZZ_rpar_.md) is called).

If the input is a pair consisting of a homogeneous ideal $I$ and an integer $v$, then the output will be the map defined by the linear system of hypersurfaces of degree $v$ which contain the projective subscheme defined by $I$.

This is identical to `toMap(I, v,1)`, while the output of `toMap(I, v, e)` will be the map defined by the linear system of hypersurfaces of degree $v$ having points of multiplicity $e$ along the projective subscheme defined by $I$.

## Parameters
- **a "matrix"** — or a [List](../../Core/type/List.md), etc.

## Returns
a [RingMap](../../Core/type/RingMap.md),

## Options
- **[Dominant](../../Cremona/symbol/Dominant.md)**

## Methods
- [`toMap(Matrix)`](#tomapmatrix)
- [`toMap(List)`](#tomaplist)
- [`toMap(Ideal)`](#tomapideal)
- [`toMap(RingMap)`](#tomapringmap)
- [`toMap(Ideal,List)`](#tomapideallist)
- [`toMap(Ideal,ZZ)`](#tomapidealzz)
- [`toMap(Ideal,ZZ,ZZ)`](#tomapidealzzzz)
