# tangentialChowForm
Higher Chow forms of a projective variety

- **Package:** [Resultants](../../Resultants/package/Resultants.md)
- **Class:** [MethodFunctionWithOptions](../../Core/type/MethodFunctionWithOptions.md)
- **Protected:** true
- **Mutable:** false
- **Defined at:** `/home/flux/.cache/yay/macaulay2/src/M2/M2/Macaulay2/packages/Resultants.m2:412:0-412:18`
- **Result type:** [RingElement](../../Core/type/RingElement.md)
- **Aliases:** `Resultants$tangentialChowForm`

## Description
For a projective variety $X\subset\mathbb{P}^n$ of dimension $k$, the $s$-th associated subvariety $Z_s(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-k-1+s,\mathbb{P}^n)$ (also called tangential Chow form) is defined to be the closure of the set of $(n-k-1+s)$-dimensional subspaces $L\subset \mathbb{P}^n$ such that $L\cap X\neq\emptyset$ and $dim(L\cap T_x(X))\geq s$ for some smooth point $x\in L\cap X$, where $T_x(X)$ denotes the embedded tangent space to $X$ at $x$. In particular, $Z_0(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-k-1,\mathbb{P}^n)$ is defined by the Chow form of $X$, while $Z_k(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-1,\mathbb{P}^n)$ is identified to the dual variety $X^{*}\subset{\mathbb{P}^n}^{*}=\mathbb{G}(0,{\mathbb{P}^n}^{*})$ via the duality of Grassmannians $\mathbb{G}(0,{\mathbb{P}^n}^{*})=\mathbb{G}(n-1,\mathbb{P}^n)$. For details we refer to the third chapter of [Discriminants, Resultants, and Multidimensional Determinants](http://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-0-8176-4771-1), by Israel M. Gelfand, Mikhail M. Kapranov and Andrei V. Zelevinsky.

The algorithm used are standard, based on projections of suitable incidence varieties. Here are some of the options available that could speed up the computation.

`Duality` Taking into account the duality of Grassmannians, one can perform the computation in $\mathbb{G}(k-s, n)$ and then passing to $\mathbb{G}(n-k-1+s, n)$. This is done by default when it seems advantageous.

`AffineChartGrass` If one of the standard coordinate charts on the Grassmannian is specified, then the internal computation is done on that chart. By default, a random chart is used. Set this to `false` to not use any chart.

`AffineChartProj` This is quite similar to `AffineChartGrass`, but it allows to specify one of the standard coordinate charts on the projective space. You should set this to `false` for working with reducible or degenerate varieties.

`AssumeOrdinary` Set this to `true` if you know that $Z_s(X)$ is a hypersurface (by default is already `true` if $s=0$).

## Parameters
- **I** — an [Ideal](../../Core/type/Ideal.md), a homogeneous ideal defining a projective variety $X=V(I)\subset\mathbb{P}^n$, say of dimension $k$
- **s** — an [ZZ](../../Core/type/ZZ.md)

## Returns
a [RingElement](../../Core/type/RingElement.md), or an [Ideal](../../Core/type/Ideal.md) (if there is more than one generator) in the coordinate ring of the Grassmannian $\mathbb{G}(n-k-1+s,\mathbb{P}^n)$ in the Plücker embedding, representing the higher associated subvariety $Z_s(X)$

## Options
- **[Variable](../../Core/symbol/Variable.md)**
- **[Duality](../../Resultants/symbol/Duality.md)**
- **[AffineChartGrass](../../Resultants/symbol/AffineChartGrass.md)** (default: `true`)
- **[AssumeOrdinary](../../Resultants/symbol/AssumeOrdinary.md)**
- **[AffineChartProj](../../Resultants/symbol/AffineChartProj.md)** (default: `true`)
- **[SingularLocus](../../Resultants/symbol/SingularLocus.md)**

## Methods
- [`tangentialChowForm(EmbeddedProjectiveVariety,ZZ,ZZ)`](#tangentialchowformembeddedprojectivevarietyzzzz)
- [`tangentialChowForm(EmbeddedProjectiveVariety,ZZ)`](#tangentialchowformembeddedprojectivevarietyzz)
- [`tangentialChowForm(Ideal,ZZ,ZZ)`](#tangentialchowformidealzzzz)
